In article <1uc2uv$8ud@agate.berkeley.edu> spp@zabriskie.berkeley.edu (Steve Pope) writes:
>
>As an anology, in the typical workplace, it is not considered
>cool to go rifling through a persons desk, file cabinets,
>etc., simply because the drawers are unlocked.
>
There are lots of things not cool to do; few of them are related to
this discussion.

A desk, file cabinet, etc, is NOT the same as a file in a file system.
I can, for instance, write a script which search the file system for
a file containing the word "foo"; you can't do that with a desk.
The script can only search those places for which I have read permission.
It's likely that it will search places that I am unaware even exist, even
after the script is run.  Arguing the ethics of searching someplace, when
you don't know you've searched it, and never will know, is a pointless
time waster.

The default for a desk or file cabinet is "no read permission".  A lock
just makes it explicit, and enforces the ban.

A file always has its permissions explicitly set.  If it's set to
"world read", then everyone has permission to read it.  It's really
that simple.  It's called "read permission" because that's exactly
what it means.  I am EXPLICITLY ALLOWED to read it.

Those of you who insist that "read permission" is meaningless are going to
have to try a little harder.  What's the point of the file permission
system, if not to allow (explicitly) the world (or group) to read
the file?  Or to prevent reading the file?


