: >what the "permission" bits are set to.  
: 
: Isn't this oxymoronic? "It doesn't matter whether you have permission,
: you don't have permission".

No.  Calling access flags "permission" is a semantic convenience but
this does not reflect reality.  In reality a user can (accidentally or
intentionally) set access flags without intending to grant permission.
Rephrase it technically and it sounds less oxymoronic:

"It doesn't matter whether the access bits allow access, you don't have
permission"

To say that typing "chmod 777" grants "permission" doesn't acknowledge
the realities of the limitations of UNIX (where only three categories
of access are available) or the level of computer novice using UNIX
systems in the modern age.

