This entire very interesting argument leaves me with some questions
about what should be in a fair computer policy.

1) misuse of printers

  OK.  There has got to be some difference between

  cat /vmunix | uuencode | lpr

  and

  lpr <some text file of about 900 lines>

2) accessing files

  To me, there are two kinds of files: 1) system files, which I assume
  I can read if group and other read permissions are set, and 2) user
  personal files, in which if there is a question of whether the user
  actually wants people to read the files, I will ask via email.

  The student in question accessed a file which is readable by group
  and other.

3) accessing files that weren't his own

  Something tells me if the student had decided to print out
  bison.texinfo or the man pages for mh, he wouldn't be facing
  suspension. 

  Are these "illegal accesses" to be excused?

  If he had previously made a copy of /etc/passwd and decided to
  print it, would he be facing suspension?  The copy would be owned by
  him.

  If another user had mailed him /etc/passwd and he printed it, what
  would happen?  That's his private mail.

4) misuse

  Some people are saying the account was opened for a specific purpose
  and he did something else, which is why he was suspended.

  This is ridiculous.  I'm not sure that permission is granted for
  news, gopher, telnetting to internet bbses, email (I mean, I keep in
  touch with friends.  Must I only use email for coursework related
  activity), anonymous ftp (surely a gross misuse of disk space, even
  though we have quotas), subscribing to mailing lists, talk, etc.
  Maybe I should be afraid.  Maybe I should get out of here.

  In the above example cited by Steve, any use of the computer
  facilities other than the intended purpose is misuse.  Since the
  student was granted an account for a class, are we to conclude that
  any work not directly related to the class would result in misuse?
  Why, working on another class' assignments from the wrong account may
  land hundreds of student in hot water!

I wish the student in question luck in his upcoming hearing.
Hopefully the committee or whoever will listen and be reasonable.


