As you are apparently *un*aware, most other OS *do* have such a feature,
and more and more versions of UNIX do too.  They're called Access Control
Lists, or ACLs.  They've been around at least since Multics in the 60s.

You can, in fact, do the following, or (in operating environments with
finer-grained permissions) more:

	(owner):	rwx
	bob:		rwx
	my-enemy:	---
	(group):	r-x
	lab-group:	r-x
	enemies-grp:	---
	(other):	--x

...which means that the owner has rwx, as does "bob"; "my-enemy" has no
permissions (even if "my-enemy" belongs to the owning group, to the
"lab-group" group, or would simply get execute permission as an "other");
members of the owning group or "lab-group" get read and execute; members
of "enemies-grp" get no rights; and everybody else gets execute permission.
