In article <C7qysn.IwG@nmrdc1.nmrdc.nnmc.navy.mil> rdc30@nmrdc1.nmrdc.nnmc.navy.mil (LCDR Michael E. Dobson) writes:
>All these arguments that the use of standard system commands that access
>the system password file during their normal operation are prima facie
>evidence that the user is granted access to *view* or *print* the contents
>of this file are non-sequiters.  For the vast majority of these commands
>the necessary checks do not reveal the information to the user.  The policy
>statement may be ill-formed or worse, but these arguments are no better.

The Unix "man" command is used to find out about the system.  (This
helps keep down the number of users running to the consultants for
advice.)  Among the things the "man" command will tell you:
"man 5 passwd" -- Tells you how the contents of /etc/passwd are
	interpreted and that /etc/passwd is generally readable
"man 3 getpwent" -- Describes a set of subroutines callable from a
	C program that read lines from /etc/passwd and split them into
	a structure for the programmer's convenience.  These subroutines
	will read *all* of the passwd file.
"man finger" -- tells you how to use the finger command.  The finger
	command will tell you: The user's login name, the user's actual
	name, the user's home directory, and the user's default shell.

There is nothing in the finger or getpwent manuals that even hints
that there are or ought to be any restrictions on who can use them,
or how they can be used.

"The Unix Programming Environment", a book frequently recommended to
beginning Unix users and programmers, mentions that /etc/passwd is
generally readable, shows examples of using the "grep" command to
display one's own passwd entry and to search for accounts without
passwords.  It also shows how to use "sed" to display the first 3 lines
of /etc/passwd.  The book takes it for granted that /etc/passwd is
readable and that there is nothing wrong with reading it.

In using Unix, talking with other Unix users, and reading Unix-related
technical newsgroups for about 8 years, I have seen that the ability
and right to read /etc/passwd is generally taken for granted.  The
users of an operating system form a culture, with different cultural
assumptions and conventions from users of other operating sytems.  One
of the basic cultural assumptions of Unix users is that they are free
to read /etc/passwd.

This is what a lot of the shouting is about.  I'd guess that those who
are taking the view that users do not have the right the read
/etc/passwd and ought to be punished for it are used to some other
operating system where the list of users is kept private.  They are
trying to apply one of their cultural assumptions to another culture
where it just does not apply.  And those arguing the a user does have
the right to read /etc/passwd are concerned because there are people
out there insisting that a man ought to be punished for what is, from
our point of view, an innocent act -- and one many of us do routinely.
-- 
David Canzi

