  What we do is to make all of the home directories readable, then make
subdirectories for course work that are not readable. Neat unix hack:
the subdirectories are OWNED by the students, with a GROUP of the
grader's group for the course, so that the professor and grader can
access the directory but no other non-superuser can. This breaks
sometimes, and relies on a sun-ism or BSD-ism that directories inherit
the group ownership of the parent, but it is really useful. Students
can then make sub-directories for their assignments that are private.

(worst feature: students are not in the graders group. If they change
the file permission on any *directory* under their course directory,
the GID bit defaults to the student GID and the grader sets up a howl.
We had to automate fixing this because we can't convince everyone to
leave their course directory protection alone.)

