========================================================
Headline: Hearing process is anti-studen
Publish Date: 05/13/1993

.RM288PT/By Laurence Kyle Pennington and Tim Wolf

Our combined experience at the Student Advocate Service forces us to 
respond to a recent article, "Student decries U hearing," (April 28) 
and the editorial "Closed hearings are authoritarian" (April 29). The 
Student Advocate Service is authorized by the University to represent 
and advocate on behalf of students who are accused of any and all 
kinds of misconduct.

First of all, Jeffrey Balfour's hearing was by no means the first 
hearing which was closed in spite of the accused student's desire 
that it be open. We have no idea where this misinformation came from, 
but it is patently false. Our office handled five cases this year 
alone with the Campus Committee on Student Behavior which were closed 
by the University representative. This activity has been going on for 
as long as we have records, and shows no sign of ending anytime soon. 
We have represented a number of students who wished to have their 
hearings held in the public domain, but whose wishes were denied.

Regarding all other commentary on the injustice of closed hearings, 
we agree. Such closed doors do not allow the monitoring of abusive 
activities against the accused by the CCSB -- activities that have 
escalated over the years. Some examples of what we feel are the more 
unethical practices include the director of Student Judicial Affairs' 
investigative interviews (read "interrogations"), during which the 
accused are neither informed of their right to counsel nor told that 
the director will be leading the prosecution against them. As far as 
we can tell, the only purpose of these interviews appears to be the 
opportunity for the accused students to incriminate themselves. 
Furthermore, in many cases the director of SJA requests no meeting at 
all and conducts no investigation whatsoever, simply accepting the 
allegations as fact and rendering sanction. This attitude that the 
accused are guilty pervades not only the Office of Student Judicial 
Affairs but also hearing panels and many colleges on campus, 
undermining the entire judicial process.

The director of Student Judicial Affairs regularly meets with the 
individuals forwarding the accusations and with the chair of the CCSB 
to offer case updates and "rules interpretations." The director 
recently provided the College of Liberal Arts information on a 
previous President's Student Behavior Review Panel decision to help 
them avoid an appeal of a CLA hearing body's decision. This action 
both violates the confidentiality of the proceedings (previous 
decisions of closed hearings are confidential) and clearly 
demonstrates the director's blatant bias, as such assistance would 
never be afforded a student.

This bias affects hearing panel decisions, due to the director's 
ability to select, train and place biased University members on the 
CCSB panel. There is no process to challenge her selections, as would 
be available in a jury selection, where either party can decide to 
exclude a potential juror for any reason. Rather, the director has 
the power to choose anyone she wants to sit on the panel. The 
director's responsibility, which according to the University is to 
"create, revise and review policies and procedures that give the 
University a fair and lawful system of student discipline," is 
actually to gather negative information and encourage and help 
prosecutors with hearing procedures in order to assure conviction. 
Remember, the director of SJA is present from the receipt of 
allegations, through the preliminary hearing, through the full 
hearing, and even through the closed door deliberations of the 
hearing panel. Having already clearly established the director's 
anti-student bias, we find it unfathomable that this is allowed to 
continue.

The hearings in question must provide what the University describes 
as fundamental fairness, a term outlined in The Regent's Appeals 
Policy and the Twin Cities Campus Appeals Procedures: The Student 
Discipline System: "A fundamentally fair hearing usually allows for 
at least . . ." the inclusion of the vague qualifier "usually" has 
allowed the University to violate every student right by stating 
"Well, usually you have the right to question adverse testimony," or 
"usually you have the right to present your case." Throughout the 
history of the Student Advocate Service, the word "usually" in the 
above document has been used to violate every one of the rights 
supposedly guaranteed to students. There is no question -- accused 
students at this institution have no rights.

The procedures themselves are unfair, and are open to constant 
reinterpretation and selective enforcement. For example, CCSB hearing 
procedures state "the Chair and Secretary will make every reasonable 
effort to assemble a panel with a majority of students." As far as we 
can tell, there has never been a hearing panel which consisted of a 
majority of students. Now, common fairness would dictate that 
students be tried by a panel of their peers -- other students. 
Clearly, to assure fairness it should be a requirement that the 
hearing panel be comprised of a majority of students.

More importantly, the accused are regularly denied the opportunity to 
have a public hearing by the University in order to cover up flagrant 
and repeated abuses of fair hearing procedures, and to extinguish the 
defendants' rights. The ability of the accused to decide whether a 
hearing is to be open or closed must be guaranteed.

Complaints have been submitted to Nick Barbatsis in his role as 
overseer of the Office of Judicial Affairs. The complaints were 
ignored and a discussion with Barbatsis degenerated into vulgar 
language on his part and unsubstantiated accusations of complaints 
against all SAS advocates.

Vice President Marvalene Hughes has been notified since her arrival 
about hearing grievance abuses, and has done nothing about them 
except to call the University lawyers for their interpretations. The 
lawyers are paid to represent the University's interests. They do not 
act impartially regarding hearing procedure issues -- rather, they 
always attempt to support the University's position regardless of the 
extent of corruption.

The administration's compliance shows unequivocally that it supports 
the current unfair and unjust system of discipline enforced at this 
University.

The Daily was right in voicing opposition to closed door hearings, 
citing that tyranny would surely follow. The problem is that it has 
already occurred.

Laurence Kyle Pennington is a student advocate. Tim Wolf is director 
of the Student Advocate Service.
-- 
