People act in one of several definitive classes. You can tell who the
system administrators are by the way they become defensive when you remind
them that you pay their salary.  You can tell who the students are by the
way they constantly remind the sysadmins that they pay their salary. 
Perhaps I can present another point of view, being someone between the two.

History constantly repeats itself.  Most people realize this by now, so i 
won't bore you with details, but here's a piece of history that you may 
want to try to repeat at UH:

The Scene:  Stevens Institute of Technology, circa 1989.

The computer center was essentially interpreting policy as general as what 
we are seeing from UH. The computer center was deciding on punishments on 
their own, based on how much they liked the person.  I wasn't actually 
around to see this, but I have seen some of the official reports on network 
violations from back then, and the staff seems to bias the reports to 
exhibit one point of view.

Students complained about this to the Vice president of information 
systems, the result of which was the creation of a board of students, named 
the Network Misuse Review Board, that reviewed network violations, and made 
recommendations for penalties.  

This board has also been actively involved in revising the policies of the 
network to help preserve freedom of speech, and make the rules more clear 
cut.  

In doing this, they system administrators became happy, because they could 
very well just refer annoyed students to this board, and students became 
happier because at least the CC wasn't arbitrarily deciding things anymore.
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Anyway, that's my history lesson for the day.  Present day, the board still
exists, and generally helps with revisions to the computing policies.
Perhaps such a board would also work at UH, it's probably worth a shot.  
Of course, i'm biased about how wonderful this board is, so take that 
opinion with a grain of salt.

While i'm opening myself to all sorts of potential flame wars, i might as
well express my opinion on what is being said. 

File Protections:
	It is understandable that user files may be protected improperly 
due to lack of knowledge on the users part.  This is a moot argument.  We 
were discussing SYSTEM files, which are generally owned by ROOT.  If 
whoever is controlling that account doesn't know about file protections, and 
the implicit privileges granted by setting them, they shouldn't be in that 
account.

	This may seem a bit harsh.  The general policy as far as Stevens is 
concerned is that if it's a system file, and you can read it, then go 
ahead.  If it's a user file, you must have explicit permission to access 
the file.

	On another note, why would anyone subject a new computer user to
Unix? It's one of the most vague operating systems ever written.  Here we
have Unix systems (for people who want access to them), and a general
VAX/VMS cluster where every student gets an account.  For ever problem I 
have with VMS being a really bad OS, at least it has a decent learning 
curve.

Wasting CPU cycles:
	Exactly where do you differentiate between a wasted CPU cycle, and 
one used to experiment and learn?  I've learned more by looking at 
everything in every system directory that I can read than by reading the 
VMS manuals that are available.  That's the way things work.

	Oh...If you want to go back to those sysadmins and give them a real 
reason to fiddle about with /etc/passwd, tell them you're writing a text 
parser to identify a username given any part of a user's real name.  It's 
an educational experiment, you're learning text parsing.

MUD's:
	As a potential law student, i've found a mud that's created to 
argue and pass rules in the imaginary world its set in, called NOMIC.  
Where exactly does UH differentiate between game playing and education?

	I've also seen way too many people fail out playing regular gaming 
type muds, so it would depend on the circumstances...

System administrators responsibility:
	There's been a lot of talk about "innocent until proven guilty".  
The system administrators should not be the police, judge, and jury.  They 
should be the police.  Their job should explicitly be to disable accounts 
and send messages detailing their reasoning to whoever is playing judge and 
jury.  Their job is to protect the system.  If you're caught doing 
something that's against their set of proscribed rules, don't blame them 
for doing their job.  Police don't wait till after you're convicted of 
murder to put you in jail, and your sysadmin shouldn't wait till after 
you're proven guilty to disable your account.

Confidentiality:
	Frankly, it's stupid.  Protecting the guilty just doesn't sit right 
somewhere with me.  However, even i'm stuck with it.  The best i've been 
able to do is to be able to talk about things in the "Student X" sense.  
It's better than nothing though.

Well, If you people at UH need any information about exactly how the NMRB 
functions, or want sample policies, send me e-mail.  

