Skimming the thread on Unix file permissions, I find it somewhat odd that this
supposedly up-and-coming operating system apparently lacks a simple, obvious
way to extend read and write access more selectively than the "world" level.
(The lion's share of my experience has been with IBM mainframes, so my lack of
intuitive feel for the fine points of Unix will hopefully be forgivable :-). )

The Access Control Facility available for IBM MVS operating systems--which many
consider rather long in the tooth--enables a user to extend read, write, and/or
execute-only access to specified files to another user or category of users,
while retaining exclusive access to the rest of his files.  Surely it should be
feasible to come up with something similar for more "modern" operating systems,
short of reinventing the wheel :-).

