In this thread, we have repeatedly seen comments like:

>people like the "hey, I can browse any darned thing I please" poster
>above really touch an open nerve with me.......it's just common
>courtesy to ask permission.

>...it simply goes against the conventions of politeness most of us
>learned as children.

These comments miss the crucial point: computers are DIFFERENT!  It is
simply not true that most of us have learned conventions as children
(or adopted common courtesies) regarding computer use.

All such arguments rely instead on analogy, going something like this:

 1. Reading other people's computer files is just like X (opening desks,
    rifling file cabinets, etc.).

 2. X is obviously rude, discourteous, unethical, or what have you.

 3. Reading other people's computer files is therefore rude,
    discourteous, unethical, etc.

The problem, as always in this type of question, is in the analogy
(step 1 above).  However obvious, strong, direct, undeniable,
effortless, and valid you might think your analogy is, rest assured
that many people, in good faith, will not agree with it.  There are
simply no good (that is, generally accepted) analogies for this type
of question.

Therefore, while you should feel free to hold your own views about
politeness, courtesy, and ethics regarding computer use, don't assume
that your views are (or ought to be) universally held.  In particular,
don't assume that if someone rejects your conclusion about computer
ethics that they must be rejecting your ethics in the 'real world'.
("Anyone who would read these files would rifle through unlocked desks
too.")  It is far more likely that they simply reject your analogy
between the computer and the real world.
