Just to add to Steve's voice here, this is a process that took us an
entire semester to do initially.  Since then, many things have come up
which could easily be classified under the policies which we helped to
revise and/or establish.  There are other concerns which have come up
along the way which were never addressed, so our "rulings" were ground-
breaking items, setting precedences for that type of case.  We continually
talk about any revisions which may be necessary to the policy, especially
after dealing with a case which doesn't match up nicely with the policies.

Probably our most interesting question came up when a faculty member
violated a rule.  While we have no jurisdiction over the faculty here,
it did raise a rather odd question and bring about a lengthy discussion
of the whole "lead by example" and other similar issues.

>In doing this, they system administrators became happy, because they could 
>very well just refer annoyed students to this board, and students became 
>happier because at least the CC wasn't arbitrarily deciding things anymore.

One thing we do not have is system privileges.  We are not sysadmin types.
We have to ask the CC for various things along the way, if they haven't sent
them to us.

>	This may seem a bit harsh.  The general policy as far as Stevens is 
>concerned is that if it's a system file, and you can read it, then go 
>ahead.  If it's a user file, you must have explicit permission to access 
>the file.

If the file is world-readable, there is some degree of an "implied consent."
Steve may disagree with me on this, but I feel that if you have the ability to
read the file, go ahead, but you may not like what you see.  If you know
what you're doing, you should know better than to leave potentially
damaging files in a private directory or take away all privileges except to
the owner.  If you don't know what you're doing, you could have a problem,
but then again, that's why they created the expression RTFM.  System level
programs and those supplied by the vendors have no business changing any
file protections, or should change them back to what you established.

From the standpoint of the original case, you WILL need a sysadmin who is
fluent in both BSD and SYS V Unix to explain how /etc/passwd works.  The
UH CC and Administration are overreacting.  If such a case were brought up
to us on the NMRB (or sometimes just NRB), unless it was an extreme waste
of paper to do such a thing, nobody would really say anything about the
SYS V file (since it's shadowed) and might complain about the BSD file
(any good Unix hacker knows how to play with the info contained therein).
MAYBE a warning letter would be sent to the person who printed the file,
and only if a whole lot of other problems were caused by the action.

While I'd be happy to explain some things, and possibly even how we've
handled some cases, Steve is far more knowledgeable about the constitution
of the group (since he and someone else wrote it :-) ) so he could
probably explain the establishment of the group easier.

