In article <1993May30.034607.27834@gmuvax2.gmu.edu> scoile@gmuvax2.gmu.edu (Steve Coile) writes:

>He could attend another school.  He could resign himself to following the
>rules.  Whatever the case, if he had no intention of following the rules, he
>should not have signed the paper.

    This is true...but it is also true that University departments, in
particular public university departments, should not be allowed to
form arbitrary rules or arbitrarily severe penalties. Unfortunately,
many rules don't seem arbitrary until they are applied; other rules
might be fair but are applied unfairly. That's where justice systems
come in -- both internal review boards and the Real World system.
   The situation described this week is one where a clearly absurd
rule (*NO* unnecessary computation or file access) has been used to
selectively punish someone for being irritating. 
   We had a similar situation here. We spent weeks drawing lines and
having meetings, giving the user an explicit contract stating precisely
what guidelines we expected him to follow, spelling out reasonable use
and what we would be looking at, and giving him several written
warnings...this allowed us to keep his account alive until the end
of the semester. In our case, though, the PENALTY for repeated
violations of reasonable use guidelines was to restrict the student's
use to coursework only! 
   Usage restrictions limiting student computer use to course work are
analagous, in my opinion, to restrictions limiting the use of the
library to books needed for a course. What a terrible idea! To carry
the analogy further, resource limits are like due dates and limits on
the number of books one student can check out...and should be flexible
at the request of the student or instructor, as ours are. 
   If a policy is interfering with education at a university, that
policy should be challenged! I do agree that breaking the policy is
not the best way...although deliberately breaking a policy is one way
to get a court case. Better to do it in an educational fashion...talk
to the faculty about the role of computers in education, talk to the
librarians about information services, talk to the students about
their computing needs, try to get some sort of community standards in
place, formally or informally. Part of the problem with computer use
is, as Barry points out, that the policies have been left to the
techies because the community as a whole is not aware of what could be
available. This is rapidly changing!

