In my (humble) opinion, people who have knowledge of how to get root access
should have fairly priviledged accounts given to them. Two reasons:

(1) It removes any incentive to hack the system: why bother??
(2) People with this sort of knowledge can be very useful (and free) weekend/
overnight operators. Ever wanted a machine rebooted on a bank holiday??

Those people I know who are students and know how to get root access are the
nerd types who can be trusted to do this kind of job properly. Many sysadmins
simply don't have the time to install all of the software they'd like to see
installed, so you can give it to these people.

It's easy to control exactly _which_ machines they would have access to.
Most students would be amazed just to have root priviledge on one little
workstation.

Finally, NFS protects file access on remote servers so you're not giving them
access to personal files, and if they do hack/screw up your system, you can
_THEN_ chuck 'em out!

