The /etc/passwd file *used* to contain encrypted passwds, UID, GID,
homedir, shell,  as well as the information you listed above in different 
fields. On some sites it still does. Most sites that want some form of 
protection use something called shadow passwds. This means the actuall 
passwd is kept in another file.

>2.   An astute "cracker" can use this information to obtain
>passwords something like 30% of the time--J.W. Smith, at Swally
>Dickens, telephone 555-1212?  Try jwssd1212, jwssd55512, etc.,
>until one combination works?

Heck, with a lot of users, it is probably just jwsmith or some such
thing. You can always just throw a dictionary at the encrypted passwd
and get a match. A shadow passwd file makes the passwd file useless
since you have to actually try to login in order to see if the passwd
works. If you have the encrypted passwd, such as at a lot of sites, then
you can simply encrypt what you think is they passwd and do a comparison
with what is actually the passwd. If there is a match, you have the
passwd without setting off any alarms from trying to login without the
passwd.

>3.   Having this information printed out on paper is somewhat less
>than useful to a "cracker", since it is easier to create & test
>passwords like this working from a computer file, but it could be
>scanned back into a file.
>
>But I thought I read that a person could download this "passwd"
>file, or at least peruse it while online.  Is that so?  In which
