>Unless they are from a non-Western civilization.

I believe the "National" in "National Association of Scholars" refers to
the United States.  As the intellectual heritage of the U.S. is part of
the "Western" (Euro-American) tradition, it is appropriate for them to
inculcate students in that tradition.  Of course, if a student is from,
say, Namibia or Papua New Guinea, then the use of "their civilisation" is
somewhat shaky, but I don't think it's incumbent on U.S. universities to
provide a Namibian or New Guinean education to those students -- they did,
after all, choose to enroll in an American university.

After all, if you chose to study abroad, say at the University of London,
you would expect the structure and philosophy of the university to be
essentially English?  Though students abroad complain bitterly about the
annoying features of their host country, few really want it to turn into
a pale replica of "back home".

None of this means, of course, that one's educational options should be
limited.  The N.A.S., so far as I can tell, champions the Western intellectual
_tradition_, not the limitation of scholarship to "Western" subjects.  For
instance, I once took a course in Zen Buddhism at the University of Illinois.
The material was entirely foreign to classical Western tradition, of course,
but the style with which the subject was approached -- in both research and
teaching -- was Western: based on dialectic, on debate, on reason.  It
was utterly different from the way Zen would be taught in real Zen monasteries,
in Tibet or Japan or Korea.  And while the traditional Eastern approach
preserved and developed the ancient teachings, the Western approach is what
really allowed it to be dispassionately analyzed, and for the cobwebs of
doctrine to be blown away from the historical truth.

So, yeah, when it comes down to it, I think the "Western intellectual
tradition" ("hands in the air, pardner, you been rustlin' cattle, or ma
name's not Diogenes"... nevermind) is worth defending.  It's certainly
much better than the age-old fallacy of putting politics or morality first,
whether that be in classical Athens or in Mao's China.  And though that
N.A.S. pamphlet was quoted by someone who I find depressingly right-wing,
I don't think the organization itself deserves the abuse you heap on it.

- David Librik
