I have two dial-up lines and two phone numbers in my house. One is
mine, one belongs to my roommate, who also has an account on my
system. Many of our users have roommates and quite a few have rommates
who are also in this program. Easiest thing in the world for a user to
start something big on their own computer and then bop over to their
roommates machine...perhaps to kill a hung process on the first line.
  Of course we do *ask* when we see two logins at the same time, and
we will check for evidence of cracking (hidden directories, logins
from out of state that are at the same time as a local login, etc) and
we have caught a couple of crackers this way. We have also caught lots
of people who have shared their passwords with friends; we make them
change their password and give them a lecture but we don't punish them
for a first offense.  We figure that sharing an account with a friend
is against our rules (mainly because we want users to be accountable)
but it is not really a crime. What's the penalty if you take out
library books for a friend or sneak a friend into a students-only
movie or something? The department has the right to limit access to
authorized users, but teaching a student how to use "talk" is better
for all of us than going nuts over the small stuff.
  Gosh, don't these sysadmins have *WORK* to do? (or netnews to read?)

 
